(4833865, 696871, 59267, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by boyd2000:</b></div><hr/><i> Excellent shot.  I love the long exposure showing the skyline, San Diego Bay, boats, blue sky and great fireworks.  Someone told me Coronado was a great place to see the July 4 fireworks since you can see several of them including Sea World, Petco Park, Del Mar Fair, up and down the coast, etc. Congratulations on the blue and for getting San Diego on the front page yet again.  I took fireworks only shots at the Del Mar (ok, now it''s San Diego County) Fair but they''re nothing compared to this. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Thanks! Yes, Coronado is. Big Bay fireworks was only 20 minutes. I remember when it finished, some people still sat there and watched Sea World''s show for a while.</td>', 'Originally posted by boyd2000: Excellent shot.  I love the long exposure showing the skyline, San Diego Bay, boats, blue sky and great fireworks.  Someone told me Coronado was a great place to see the July 4 fireworks since you can see several of them including Sea World, Petco Park, Del Mar Fair, up and down the coast, etc. Congratulations on the blue and for getting San Diego on the front page yet again.  I took fireworks only shots at the Del Mar (ok, now it''s San Diego County) Fair but they''re nothing compared to this.  Thanks! Yes, Coronado is. Big Bay fireworks was only 20 minutes. I remember when it finished, some people still sat there and watched Sea World''s show for a while.', '2008-07-19 13:21:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(4834709, 695769, 67697, '<td valign="top">Looks like you have a lot of beautiful places to explore up there!  I learned a new word today lol.  This looks so cool and fresh.  I love the pop of green too.</td>', 'Looks like you have a lot of beautiful places to explore up there!  I learned a new word today lol.  This looks so cool and fresh.  I love the pop of green too.', '2008-07-20 01:33:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(4834725, 697013, 24896, '<td valign="top">Hi Rachel, Very interesting location for this pic. Lots of potential for composition here. I am guessing the road and fence is what youre trying to capture here.<br/>With that dull bit of sky up in the top left I think I would have just left it totally out of the picture. Maybe a square crop with this one might help (and gives the voters a BIGGER picture to look at too). Rotate the pic till the fence runs almost corner to corner, so the frame is filled with fence and road. Then whenever I get a pic with lots of dull green/brown mix I start thinking B&amp;W or really crank up the saturation and contrast give it some POP! Or try a lower perspective shooting from close to the bottom of the fence. This shot could have used a tripod too. I think it is probably a little soft due to some low light (slow shutter speed) and camera shake. Playing with a faster aperture might have helped some too (try shooting in A mode "aperture priority"). It is almost like shooting in auto but give you total creative control over depth of field (DOF) You picked a very interesting location to shoot, this shot just ended up a little soft and kinda flat.<br/> <br/>Start posting your exif info too. I will check up on your photos when I have the chance to comment, and the exif would help me to make other suggestions for you. If you have any questions or comments please feel free to PM me. Scott<br/> <br/></td>', 'Hi Rachel, Very interesting location for this pic. Lots of potential for composition here. I am guessing the road and fence is what youre trying to capture here.With that dull bit of sky up in the top left I think I would have just left it totally out of the picture. Maybe a square crop with this one might help (and gives the voters a BIGGER picture to look at too). Rotate the pic till the fence runs almost corner to corner, so the frame is filled with fence and road. Then whenever I get a pic with lots of dull green/brown mix I start thinking B&W or really crank up the saturation and contrast give it some POP! Or try a lower perspective shooting from close to the bottom of the fence. This shot could have used a tripod too. I think it is probably a little soft due to some low light (slow shutter speed) and camera shake. Playing with a faster aperture might have helped some too (try shooting in A mode "aperture priority"). It is almost like shooting in auto but give you total creative control over depth of field (DOF) You picked a very interesting location to shoot, this shot just ended up a little soft and kinda flat. Start posting your exif info too. I will check up on your photos when I have the chance to comment, and the exif would help me to make other suggestions for you. If you have any questions or comments please feel free to PM me. Scott ', '2008-07-20 01:53:32.000000', NULL, 0),
